W    carrier  stride(Cold)  span_bytes  access  carrier==access?
1    u8      1             1           u8     yes
3    u8      3             2           u16    NO
5    u8      5             2           u16    NO
7    u8      7             2           u16    NO
8    u8      8             2           u16    NO
9    u16     9             2           u16    yes
13   u16     13            3           u32    NO
16   u16     16            3           u32    NO
17   u32     17            3           u32    yes
23   u32     23            4           u32    yes
25   u32     25            4           u32    yes
31   u32     31            5           u64    NO
32   u32     32            5           u64    NO
33   u64     33            5           u64    yes
47   u64     47            7           u64    yes
57   u64     57            8           u64    yes
64   u64     64            9           u128   NO

across W = 1..=64: carrier and access width disagree for 28 of 64 widths
closed form floor((W+6)/8)+1 vs exhaustive phase scan, W = 1..=1024: 0 mismatches

recoverability, checked rather than argued:
  from carrier u8   : the 8 widths behind it need access widths [8, 16] -> NOT recoverable
  from carrier u16  : the 8 widths behind it need access widths [16, 32] -> NOT recoverable
  from carrier u32  : the 16 widths behind it need access widths [32, 64] -> NOT recoverable
  from carrier u64  : the 32 widths behind it need access widths [64, 128] -> NOT recoverable
  from the extent  : W is carried directly, so access = f(W) is recoverable for every W

(b) a too-narrow access is wrong only when the truncated bits are non-zero.
  values 0..64 (a small-value test)  -> 0 of 64 wrong with a 2-byte access
  values with the top bit set        -> 32 of 64 wrong with a 2-byte access
  all bits set                       -> 32 of 64 wrong with a 2-byte access

so the catching check is itself blind to a test that uses small values.
only a test whose data fills the declared width exercises the truncation.
